  <ferc:OrderNumber id="Tag752" contextRef="C64" unitRef="Pure" decimals="0">8</ferc:OrderNumber>
  <ferc:StatementAboutImportantChangesDuringTheQuarterYearTextBlock id="Tag753" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;1. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;2. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;3. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;4. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;5. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;6. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;7. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;8. The wage rate for Hampshire's management employees reflected a 1.65% increase, effective April 1, 2025&amp;#160;and non-management employees reflected a 3.0% increase, effective January 1, 2025.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;9. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;10. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;11.&amp;#160;On March 12, 2025, Washington Gas received FERC approval of its amended tariff, which allows Hampshire to bill Washington Gas for the actual settled costs of AROs. During the year ended December 31, 2025, this resulted in settled costs of $656,712.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;12. None&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;13. Not applicable&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:StatementAboutImportantChangesDuringTheQuarterYearTextBlock>
  <ferc:AttestationName id="Tag754" contextRef="C1">Colin Bond</ferc:AttestationName>
  <ferc:AttestationTitle id="Tag755" contextRef="C1">Vice President and Controller</ferc:AttestationTitle>
  <ferc:CorporateOfficerCertificationSignature id="Tag756" contextRef="C1">Colin Bond</ferc:CorporateOfficerCertificationSignature>
  <ferc:AttestationDate id="Tag757" contextRef="C1">2026-04-17</ferc:AttestationDate>
  <ferc:FormType id="Tag758" contextRef="C1">2-A</ferc:FormType>
  <ferc:RespondentLegalName id="Tag759" contextRef="C1">Hampshire Gas Company</ferc:RespondentLegalName>
  <ferc:CompanyIdentifier id="Tag760" contextRef="C1">C001977</ferc:CompanyIdentifier>
  <ferc:ReportYear id="Tag761" contextRef="C1" unitRef="Pure" decimals="0">2025</ferc:ReportYear>
  <ferc:ReportPeriod id="Tag762" contextRef="C1">Q4</ferc:ReportPeriod>
  <ferc:AddressOfPrincipalOfficeAtEndOfPeriod id="Tag763" contextRef="C1">Hampshire Gas Drive, Romney, WV 26757-7260</ferc:AddressOfPrincipalOfficeAtEndOfPeriod>
  <ferc:NameOfContactPerson id="Tag764" contextRef="C1">Mike Rafferty</ferc:NameOfContactPerson>
  <ferc:TitleOfContactPerson id="Tag765" contextRef="C1">Manager, Financial and Regulatory Reporting</ferc:TitleOfContactPerson>
  <ferc:AddressOfContactPerson id="Tag766" contextRef="C1">6801 Industrial Rd, Springfield, VA, 22151</ferc:AddressOfContactPerson>
  <ferc:TelephoneOfContactPerson id="Tag767" contextRef="C1">202-624-6282</ferc:TelephoneOfContactPerson>
  <ferc:SubmissionType id="Tag768" contextRef="C1">O</ferc:SubmissionType>
  <ferc:ReportDate id="Tag769" contextRef="C1">2025-12-31</ferc:ReportDate>
  <ferc:NameOfOfficerWithCustodyOfCorporateBooks id="Tag770" contextRef="C1">Colin Bond</ferc:NameOfOfficerWithCustodyOfCorporateBooks>
  <ferc:OfficerTitle id="Tag771" contextRef="C1">Vice President and Controller</ferc:OfficerTitle>
  <ferc:AddressWhereCorporateBooksAreKept id="Tag772" contextRef="C1">1000 Maine Ave SW #600, Washington, DC 20024</ferc:AddressWhereCorporateBooksAreKept>
  <ferc:StateOfIncorporation id="Tag773" contextRef="C1">WV</ferc:StateOfIncorporation>
  <ferc:IncorporationDate id="Tag774" contextRef="C1">1963-06-26</ferc:IncorporationDate>
  <ferc:MannerAndExtentOfCorporateControl id="Tag775" contextRef="C1">At December 31, 2025, WGL Holdings, Inc. (WGL), an indirect wholly owned subsidiary of AltaGas Ltd., owned and controlled 100% of the outstanding common stock of the Hampshire Gas Company (Hampshire). The financial statements of Hampshire are compiled and generated by the Controller's Department of Washington Gas Light Company (Washington Gas) where common services of accounting, tax, financial reporting, etc. are provided for all the subsidiaries of WGL Holdings, Inc. Hampshire does not have a Chief Financial Officer. Accordingly, the Controller of Washington Gas performs similar functions as a Principal Accounting Officer.</ferc:MannerAndExtentOfCorporateControl>
  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="Tag776" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in 7.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note 1: Accounting Policies &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in 7.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Nature of Operations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Hampshire Gas Company (&amp;#8220;we,&amp;#8221; &amp;#8220;our,&amp;#8221; &amp;#8220;Hampshire,&amp;#8221; or the "Company" refers to Hampshire Gas Company) operates and owns full and partial interests in underground natural gas storage facilities, including pipeline delivery facilities located in and around Hampshire County, West Virginia. The Company provides storage services exclusively to Washington Gas Light Company (Washington Gas). Both the Company and Washington Gas are regulated subsidiaries of WGL Holdings, Inc. (WGL), an indirect wholly owned subsidiary of AltaGas Ltd. (AltaGas). The Company operates under a &amp;#8220;pass-through&amp;#8221; cost of service-based tariff approved by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) and adjusts its billing rates to Washington Gas on a periodic basis to account for changes in its investment in utility plant and associated expenses. Operating revenue for Hampshire is recognized for the amount the Company has the right to invoice to Washington Gas, which is based on the actual operating costs incurred by Hampshire plus a return on investment (ROI).&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in 7.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Basis of Accounting&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;The financial statements are presented in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) as set forth in its applicable uniform system of accounts (USofA) and published accounting releases which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America (GAAP). Financial accounting and reporting differences between FERC and GAAP are principally related to account classifications such as but not limited to: 1) deferred tax assets and liabilities are presented as gross assets and liabilities for FERC purposes, but are presented net under GAAP, 2) both the service and non-service components of net periodic pension and postretirement benefit costs are presented as operating expenses for FERC, while under GAAP the non-service component of net periodic benefit cost is presented outside of operating income, and 3) operating lease assets and liabilities are presented using the existing FERC balance sheet accounts for capital leases, but for rate making purposes, only rent expense is included in cost of service, consistent with FERC&amp;#8217;s tariff requirements.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in 7.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Use of Estimates in the Preparation of Financial Statements&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;These statements include certain estimates and assumptions made by management regarding: (i) reported assets and liabilities, (ii) disclosed contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements, and (iii) reported revenues and expenses during the reporting period. Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Utility Plant&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Utility plant is stated at original cost, including labor, materials, taxes, and overhead costs incurred during the construction period. When the Company retires depreciable utility plant and equipment, it charges the associated original costs to "Accum. Provision for Depr., Amort., Depl.&amp;#8221;. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Costs of replacement and betterments that extend the useful life of property, plant, and equipment are also capitalized. The cost of maintenance and repairs, which do not extend the useful life or increase the expected output of the asset, are charged to expense as incurred. Depreciation applicable to utility gas plant in service primarily uses a straight-line method over the estimated remaining life of the plant.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Leases&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Hampshire leases a building and tower space for wireless communications equipment. The leases are classified as operating leases. Right of use (ROU) assets represent the right to use an underlying asset for the lease term, and lease liabilities represent the obligation to make lease payments arising from the lease. ROU assets and lease liabilities are recognized at the commencement date based on the present value of lease payments over the lease term; however, for rate making purposes, these balance sheet amounts are not included in the rate base. The Company uses the rate implicit in the lease to determine the present value of the lease payment when the rate is readily determinable, or we use an incremental borrowing rate. At December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company had a ROU asset of $129,675 and $186,469, respectively, which was included in &amp;#8220;Utility Plant&amp;#8221;, a current lease liability of $50,735 and $64,368, respectively, in &amp;#8220;Obligation under Capital Leases-Current&amp;#8221;, and non-current lease liability of $74,395 and $118,461, respectively, in &amp;#8220;Obligation Under Capital Leases-Noncurrent&amp;#8221;, related to our operating leases. Lease expense is recognized on a straight-line basis over the lease term. Total lease expense for both years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024, was $65,006, which was recorded in operation and maintenance expense and included in the cost of service for rate making. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 0in; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;Additionally, Washington Gas has an agreement to purchase all of the storage services of&amp;#160;Hampshire. This arrangement is deemed to be an operating lease because Washington Gas has the right and ability to direct the operation of&amp;#160;Hampshire&amp;#8217;s&amp;#160;storage facilities. Refer to Revenues &amp;amp; Billing Correction Related to Asset Retirement Obligations (AROs) below as well as Note 5. Related Party Transactions.&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: normal; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Revenues &amp;amp; Billing Correction Related to Asset Retirement Obligations (AROs)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Hampshire provides storage services exclusively to Washington Gas. Both the Company and Washington Gas are regulated subsidiaries of WGL. The Company operates under a &amp;#8220;pass-through&amp;#8221; cost of service-based tariff approved by FERC and adjusts its billing rates to Washington Gas on a periodic basis to account for changes in its investment in utility plant and associated expenses. Operating revenue for Hampshire is recognized for the amount the Company has the right to invoice to Washington Gas, which is based on the actual operating costs incurred by Hampshire plus a return on investment (ROI).&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;On June 25, 2024, Hampshire made a one-time refund to Washington Gas of $9,514,968, including interest, to correct a historical error related to the billing of costs related to AROs, as Hampshire did not have FERC approval to bill for ARO related amounts in its tariff.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;Of the $9,514,968 refund, $8,555,884 relates to 2023 and prior.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;On March 12, 2025, Washington Gas received FERC approval of its amended tariff, which allows Hampshire to bill Washington Gas for the actual settled costs of AROs. As such, Hampshire records all ARO accretion and depreciation expense to Other Regulatory Assets, and bills Washington Gas for these costs, as well as for any gains and losses, when AROs settle. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: normal; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Regulated Operations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Hampshire accounts for its regulated operations in accordance with the accounting requirements of the FERC as set forth in its applicable USofA and published accounting releases. This standard includes accounting principles for companies whose rates are determined by independent third-party regulators. When setting rates, regulators may require the Company to record costs as expense in different periods than may be appropriate for unregulated enterprises. When this occurs, Hampshire defers the associated costs as assets (regulatory assets) on its balance sheet and records them as expenses on its income statement as it collects the revenues designed to recover these costs through customers&amp;#8217; rates.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;Further, regulators can also impose liabilities upon a company for refunds to customers and for amounts collected from customers for costs that are expected to be incurred in the future (regulatory liabilities).&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;Regulatory assets are being recovered as components of cost of service. Although recovery of these amounts is not guaranteed, the Company believes that these costs meet the requirements for deferral as regulatory assets as defined by the FERC. If the Company determined that the amounts included as regulatory assets were not recoverable, a charge to income would immediately be required to the extent of the unrecovered amounts.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;At December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company had regulatory asset balances of $4,391,843 and $4,690,655, and regulatory liability balances of $1,820,647 and $1,724,752, respectively. The regulatory asset balances primarily relate to AROs. In addition, the regulatory asset balances also include deferred income taxes and tax reform remeasurement. The regulatory liability balances primarily relate to pension, other post-retirement benefits, and deferred income taxes and tax reform remeasurement.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: normal; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: normal; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;We recognize deferred income tax assets and liabilities for all temporary differences between the financial statement basis and the tax basis of assets and liabilities computed on a separate company basis. Regulatory assets or liabilities have been established for the excess deferred income taxes primarily as a result of the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. Plant related excess deferred income taxes are collected from or payable to the customer using the Average Rate Assumption Method as required by the Internal Revenue Code. Non-plant related excess deferred income taxes are collected from or payable to the customer over a ten-year period. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: 115%; tab-stops: .25in .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 10.0pt; line-height: normal; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 6.5in 7.0in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'times new roman', times; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;Our asset retirement obligations include the costs to plug storage wells upon retirement, as well as the cost to cut, cap, and purge the associated pipeline system used to inject and withdraw natural gas. We record the estimated retirement costs associated with depreciation and accretion to Other Regulatory Assets, and bill Washington Gas for these costs, as well as for any gains and losses, when AROs settle.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 0in; line-height: normal; tab-stops: .5in 1.0in 1.5in 2.0in 2.5in 3.0in 3.5in 4.0in 4.5in 5.0in 5.5in 6.0in 7.0in 7.5in; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman', times;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 44.66%; height: 162px;" border="0"&gt;

